// Tailscale access policy for the a11y-witness fleet.
//
// PASTE THIS into the admin console under Access Controls. It lives here rather than only in the console
// because it is infrastructure configuration, and this project's rule is that configuration nobody can
// review or diff is configuration that drifts — the same argument that put the fleet in `inventory.yml`
// and the lab jobs in a catalogue.
//
// ## Why this exists at all
//
// The four capture workers serve HTTP on :8765 with **no authentication of any kind**. That is deliberate
// and documented (`packages/worker-fleet/ansible/README.md`): a mutating route on an unauthenticated
// worker would be remote code execution on every box, which is why the fleet is managed over SSH instead.
//
// It was a safe design while "on the LAN" was the trust boundary. Since 2026-08-23 the boundary is "on the
// tailnet", and Tailscale's DEFAULT policy is allow-all — every device reaching every port. So without
// this file, adding a phone to the tailnet gives that phone the ability to drive a screen reader on four
// machines. This narrows it back to what the work actually needs.
{
  // Tag the subnet router. A tag is what `autoApprovers` and the rules below can refer to; an untagged
  // device is identified by its owner, which changes if the machine is ever re-registered.
  "tagOwners": {
    "tag:a11y-fleet": ["autogroup:admin"],
  },

  // Approve advertised routes automatically, INSIDE this prefix and no wider.
  //
  // Without it, every machine added to `inventory.yml` needs a visit to the admin console before it is
  // reachable — a manual step in a workflow that repeats, which is exactly the shape this project keeps
  // removing. Scoped to REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS/24 so the router can only ever auto-approve routes on the home LAN;
  // it cannot approve a route to something else and quietly become a path there.
  "autoApprovers": {
    "routes": {
      "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS/24": ["tag:a11y-fleet"],
    },
  },

  // Default DENY. Everything not listed below is refused.
  //
  // Tailscale's starter policy is a single allow-all rule, and leaving it is the whole risk: it means the
  // tailnet's security is "every device I ever add is trustworthy forever", which is a promise nobody can
  // keep. Each rule below names what it is for, so a future reader can tell a deliberate hole from an
  // accident.
  "acls": [
    // Your own devices reach the capture workers on the worker port only.
    //
    // Port-scoped rather than `*:*` on purpose: the workers also expose a diagnostics endpoint and a
    // Windows box has plenty else listening. This is the port the tooling uses and the only one it needs.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": [
        "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:8765",
        "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:8765",
        "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:8765",
        "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:8765",
      ],
    },

    // SSH to the lab, the control container, the hypervisor and the AI server.
    //
    // `lab:job`, `lab:fetch` and `lab:stop` are Ansible over SSH from a laptop, so this is the rule that
    // makes working away from home possible at all. The control container holds the fleet key (ADR 0012),
    // which is why this is deliberately narrow: SSH, and nothing else.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": [
        "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:22",
        "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:22",
        "REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:22",
        "tag:a11y-fleet:22",
      ],
    },

    // The AI server, on whatever it serves. Left broad on purpose and flagged as such: it is not part of
    // this project, its ports are not this file's business, and pretending to know them would be a rule
    // that looks precise and is guesswork. Narrow it when its actual ports are known.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["REDACTED-INTERNAL-ADDRESS:*"],
    },
  ],

  // WAKE-ON-LAN DOES NOT WORK THROUGH THIS, and no rule here can change that.
  //
  // `fleet:wake` sends a magic packet, which is a LAN broadcast — it does not cross a tailnet, because a
  // subnet router forwards unicast to specific addresses. Waking a sleeping worker still needs something
  // already on the LAN to send it, which the control container is. Recorded here because the symptom
  // ("wake does nothing when I am away") looks like an ACL problem and is not one.
}
